---
/**
 * /privacy — the trust anchor.
 *
 * Every claim on this page was checked against the code before it was written,
 * and the page says so about the one exception rather than rounding down to
 * "no third parties": `faviconUrl()` in `lib/links.ts` is rendered by
 * `components/ToolList.astro` and `pages/tools/[slug].astro`, so the tool pages
 * really do hit logo.dev. A privacy page that is wrong about its own build is
 * worth less than no page at all; changing the logo host means changing this
 * page in the same commit.
 *
 * The storage paragraph is the same rule applied a second time. This page used
 * to say the site "writes nothing to local storage", which stopped being true
 * the moment the theme toggle shipped, so it now names the one key instead.
 * Anything that reaches for `localStorage` has to come back and edit this.
 *
 * The takedown paragraph is the third. It used to end "That is the only contact
 * route on this site", which stopped being true the moment /contact shipped, so
 * it now says why an issue is still the route it asks for rather than claiming
 * there is no other one. It grew a second half when /library started rehosting
 * the pictures attached to a saved post: the same rule that made the /sites
 * gallery need a takedown route makes a post card need one, and the route is
 * the same route rather than a second one.
 *
 * That paragraph names three things and no more, because three things are what
 * the data carries: who posted it (`Post.author` and `Post.handle`), the
 * original post (the entry's `url`), and the day the link was saved
 * (`saved_date`). **It deliberately does not claim a retrieval date for a
 * picture.** `saved_date` is the day the pipeline first published the entry,
 * and the four pictures on the site today were fetched by a later backfill, so
 * "the day I fetched this file" is a fact nothing here stores. Storing it would
 * be the honest way to make that claim; claiming it from a field that means
 * something else would not.
 *
 * The newsletter is the fourth, and it is the first one written before the thing
 * it describes exists. Both the paragraph about it and the sentence it would
 * falsify are gated on `NEWSLETTER_ACTION`, the same string the signup block
 * itself is gated on, so the page cannot describe a form the site does not have
 * and cannot claim there are no forms once it does. One line in `lib/site.ts`
 * moves the control and this page together, which is the only arrangement that
 * survives someone turning the newsletter on months from now without reading
 * this comment.
 *
 * The analytics section is the fifth, and it is the one that came due. This
 * page used to say there was no analytics here, and promised that anything
 * which counted pages would rewrite it in the same commit. That commit is this
 * one: Vercel Web Analytics now ships from `layouts/Base.astro`.
 *
 * The claim that had to be re-checked rather than assumed was the one directly
 * below it. Both of the analytics URLs are same-origin paths on this domain
 * (`/_vercel/insights/script.js` and `/_vercel/insights/event`), so **"the one
 * outside request" survived intact** and still means what it says. Had the
 * script loaded from a Vercel host instead, that section would have had to name
 * a second one in this same commit. Check that pair again before changing how
 * analytics is loaded.
 *
 * The videos are the sixth, and they are the first thing on this site a reader
 * can choose to load from someone else. `components/VideoFacade.astro` renders
 * a saved video as the still this repository committed plus a play control, and
 * builds the YouTube player only on a press — so the section below had to stop
 * saying "nothing else loads from anyone else" flat and start saying **when**.
 * Scoping it to the page load is what keeps both halves true at once: the site
 * still makes exactly one outside request of its own, on every page, and a page
 * with no video on it still cannot make a second one however hard you press it.
 *
 * The posts are the seventh, and they are the first one that made this page
 * worse rather than longer. It used to say the post cards were drawn from files
 * in this repository specifically so a reader would not be handed to x.com, and
 * that was true and was the better arrangement for a reader. Aayush reversed it
 * with the trade in front of him (VET-67): the hand-built cards looked wrong,
 * and the wall is what people actually look at. So `/library/kind/post` now
 * loads X's widget factory on page load — not on a press, which is the video's
 * arrangement and not this one — and every card on it becomes an iframe of
 * theirs.
 *
 * Three sections had to move together for that, which is what "in the same
 * commit" means in practice. The one outside request became **two pages that
 * load from somewhere else**, because "one" was the number and it is not any
 * more. The storage paragraph grew a sentence, because what X puts in a
 * reader's browser from inside its own frame is filed under X and this page
 * should not imply the theme key is the whole story. And the takedown section
 * stopped claiming the cards are a copy, because on that page they are not —
 * the copy is on the entry's own page, which is exactly what the card now
 * points at.
 *
 * `scripts/validate-schema.mjs` holds the load-bearing half: the Twitter hosts
 * may appear on `/library/kind/post` and must appear there, and may not appear
 * on any other page in `dist/`. So the sentence "every other page here loads
 * nothing from anyone" is a build failure rather than a promise.
 *
 * Underneath all seven is one rule: **every claim here is a claim about today's
 * build, and it says so.** A never-ever about analytics or third parties is a
 * promise a future commit can turn into a lie, and a privacy page that has gone
 * false is worse than none. So the page states what is true now and names the
 * commit that would have to change it, which is a claim that stays honest
 * whichever way the site goes.
 *
 * Not in the nav or the section manifest on purpose: it is a footer page, and
 * it sits in the colophon row beside /about and /contact.
 *
 * The prose shell is `styles/global.css › .doc`, shared with those two. It
 * lived here as a scoped block until there were three pages that had to look
 * like siblings.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { NEWSLETTER_ACTION } from "../lib/site";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy · Aayush Manchanda"
  description="What this site collects, what it loads from elsewhere, and how to get a screenshot of your site taken down."
>
  <header>
    <h1 class="page-title">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Short version: this site is a pile of static files. It counts how many
      people open a page, and it has no idea who any of them are. Two pages
      borrow something from somebody else, and they are named below.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="doc">
    <section>
      <h2 class="doc__head mono">What I collect</h2>
      {
        NEWSLETTER_ACTION ? (
          <p>
            Almost nothing you hand over, and the one exception is a box you
            have to fill in yourself. There are no accounts and no comments. The
            only form on the site is the newsletter signup on the front page,
            and what happens to what you type into it is written out below. The
            site also counts page views, which is the next section.
          </p>
        ) : (
          <p>
            Nothing you hand over. There are no accounts, no forms, no comments,
            no newsletter box. There is nothing here for you to fill in, and
            nowhere for it to go if there were. The site does count page views,
            and that is the next section.
          </p>
        )
      }
      <p>
        The fonts are bundled into the build and served from this domain, so
        reading a page here does not tell a font CDN that you were reading it.
      </p>
      <p>
        It writes one thing to local storage, and only after you ask it to: the
        theme. If you press the theme button in the footer, the site saves the
        word <em>light</em>, <em>dark</em> or <em>system</em> so the next page
        you open is the colour you picked. That is the whole entry. It never
        leaves your browser, nothing reads it but this site, and clearing your
        site data forgets it. Until you press it, nothing is stored at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        That covers what this site stores. It does not cover what X stores
        inside its own embeds on <a href="/library/kind/post">the posts page</a>,
        which is filed under X and not under me. The section below is what that
        page costs you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="doc__head mono">Counting page views</h2>
      <p>
        Since 26 August 2026 this site counts page views with <a
          href="https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/privacy-policy"
          rel="noopener nofollow"
        >Vercel Web Analytics</a>. I wanted to know which pages people actually
        read, because that is most of what tells me whether any of this was
        worth someone&rsquo;s time.
      </p>
      <p>
        There are no cookies, and nothing is written to your browser. You are
        not given an ID, and nothing recorded here can be joined up with your
        visit to any other site. Vercel counts a visit by hashing the request it
        already received, and that hash is discarded after 24 hours.
      </p>
      <p>
        What gets recorded is the page you opened, the site that sent you, your
        browser and operating system, whether you are on a phone, a tablet or a
        desktop, and the country, region and city the request came from. That is
        the whole list. It reaches me as totals: this page got this many views
        this week, most of them from that referrer. There is no view of it that
        is one person, and I could not build one.
      </p>
      <p>
        The script is served from this domain rather than from an analytics
        company, and the counts it sends go to the same place. So counting page
        views did not add a host to the list below: it added a script, and the
        script is mine. Vercel holds the numbers, which is the company already
        serving you these pages. If that ever moves to someone else, the new
        name lands here on the commit that moves it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="doc__head mono">The two pages that load from somewhere else</h2>
      <p>
        The tool pages are the exception and I would rather name it than claim a
        clean sweep. Each tool row shows the logo of that tool&rsquo;s own
        website, and those come from <a href="https://logo.dev" rel="noopener nofollow"
        >logo.dev</a>, a logo API, instead of my server. So loading <a
          href="/tools">/tools</a> or any tool page makes a request to logo.dev
        with the tool&rsquo;s domain in the URL, and logo.dev sees your IP
        address the way any host does when your browser fetches an image. The
        request is sent with no referrer, so it does not carry which page you
        were on. The URL carries a publishable key that identifies my logo.dev
        account for quota, not you.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/library/kind/post">The posts page</a> is the other one, and it
        is the heavier of the two. Every post on it is X&rsquo;s own embed, so
        opening that page loads a script from X and turns each card into a frame
        of theirs. X sees your IP address, sees that the frame is on this site,
        and whatever happens inside it is theirs rather than mine. Each embed is
        marked do-not-track, which asks X not to use the visit to tailor
        anything, and asking is all I can do from here. If that trade is not one
        you want, the page for any single post is on this domain and loads
        nothing from anyone: the words are a copy I saved, and every card has a
        <em>notes</em> link that goes straight there.
      </p>
      <p>
        I used to draw those cards myself from the saved copy, and this page
        used to say why that was better. It was better for your privacy and it
        looked wrong: a hand-built imitation of a post is uncanny in a way the
        real one is not. So I took the real embed and moved the honest version
        one press away, which is the trade written out rather than quietly made.
      </p>
      <p>
        The saved videos are the outside request you can make yourself, and
        nothing loads from YouTube until you press play. A video on <a
          href="/library/kind/video">the videos page</a> shows a still I copied
        into this repository when I saved it, the same way the screenshots work,
        so the page arrives with a picture from this domain and no player in it.
        Press play and the still is replaced by one, and that is the moment your
        browser first talks to Google: the player comes from
        youtube-nocookie.com, which holds off the usual cookies until something
        is actually played, and is still Google either way. They get your IP
        address and the name of this site, not the page you were reading, and
        anything stored after that is stored under their name rather than mine.
        On a page with no video on it there is nothing to press, so there is
        nothing to load.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the whole list: logo.dev on the tool pages, X on the posts page,
        YouTube if you press play. Every other page here loads nothing from
        anyone, and a third host would be named in this section on the commit
        that added it.
      </p>
    </section>

    {
      NEWSLETTER_ACTION && (
        <section>
          <h2 class="doc__head mono">The newsletter</h2>
          <p>
            The signup box on the front page is the one thing here you can type
            into. Nothing loads from Buttondown to draw it or watch it: it is
            plain HTML served from this domain, so the section above still
            stands. Nothing goes anywhere until you press Subscribe.
          </p>
          <p>
            When you do, your browser sends your address to <a
              href="https://buttondown.com"
              rel="noopener nofollow"
            >Buttondown</a>, which runs the list and sends the issues. Usually
            you stay on this page and the box says it went through; Buttondown
            then emails you a link to confirm. If Buttondown wants to check
            something first, a CAPTCHA or an address it does not like, your
            browser goes to their page so they can ask you directly. Either way
            Buttondown gets your address, and sees your IP the way any host does
            when a browser talks to it. This site never receives either. There
            is no server here to receive them with.
          </p>
          <p>
            Your address is for sending you the newsletter and nothing else. I do
            not sell it, and I do not upload it anywhere. Every issue has an
            unsubscribe link at the bottom, and pressing it takes you off the
            list without going through me.
          </p>
        </section>
      )
    }

    <section>
      <h2 class="doc__head mono">Server logs</h2>
      <p>
        The site is hosted on Vercel, and Vercel keeps its own standard request
        logs, same as any host. I do not read them, I do not analyse them, and I
        have not wired anything to them. They are a side effect of being on the
        internet, not something I set up to learn about you. They are also
        separate from the page counting above, which is a script running in the
        page and not something read back out of a log.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="doc__head mono">Screenshots, saved posts and video stills</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/sites">/sites</a> gallery is screenshots of other
        people&rsquo;s websites. A pipeline takes them automatically when I save
        a link, and
        every one of them gets a page that links out to the site it came from.
        If one of them is yours and you want it gone, open an issue at <a
          href="https://github.com/aayushmanchanda2"
          rel="noopener">github.com/aayushmanchanda2</a> and I will take it
        down. You do not have to explain why. An issue is the route I would
        rather you used for this, because it leaves a record that the request
        was made and answered. If you would rather not open one, <a
          href="/contact">/contact</a> has my address.
      </p>
      <p>
        The saved posts work the same way underneath. When I save something from
        x.com, the pipeline copies the words and any pictures into this
        repository, and the page for that post is drawn from those files. What
        sits on <a href="/library/kind/post">the posts page</a> is X&rsquo;s own
        embed instead, which is the trade in the section above: their card on
        the wall, my copy on the page it points at. The post is still the
        poster&rsquo;s either way. Every one of them names who wrote it and links
        back to the original, and its row on <a href="/library">/library</a> carries
        the date I saved it. If one of them is yours, the same issue link works
        and the same answer applies.
      </p>
      <p>
        A saved video is the same arrangement with one picture in it. What sits
        on the page before you press play is YouTube&rsquo;s own poster frame
        for that video, fetched once when I saved the link and stored here, so
        that showing you what you are about to watch does not cost you a request
        to Google. If it is your video and you would rather I did not, the issue
        link above takes it down too.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="doc__head mono">Who owns what</h2>
      <p>
        My writing and my notes are mine. The screenshots and anything I quote
        belong to whoever made the thing they are of. If you want to reuse
        something I wrote, go ahead. A link back is plenty.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
